Confirms the handover. `ios_base::_Callfns(event)`: walk the singly linked callback list whose head is `_Calls` at `+0x2C` and call each node's function with the event, the stream and the node's index.

    for (node = this->_Calls; node; node = node->_Next)
        node->_Pfn(event, *this, node->_Index);

The node layout falls straight out of the loop - `+0` `_Next`, `+4` `_Index`, `+8` `_Pfn` - and the callback is `__cdecl`: three pushes and `add esp, 0xC` at `0xB94B08`, in the order `(event, this, index)`. That is `typedef void (__cdecl *event_callback)(event, ios_base&, int)` exactly.

`__thiscall`, one stack argument, `ret 4`, nothing returned. **It has exactly one caller in the whole image**, `std::ios_base::_Tidy` (`0x794B15`), which passes the literal 0 - `erase_event`. So no registered callback in this game is ever told about `copyfmt_event` or `imbue_event`; the list is only ever run at teardown.

Confirms the handover. `_Lockit::_Lockit(int _Kind)`: store the kind at `+0`, and if it is below 4 enter the critical section at `0x174C878 + kind*0x18`.

    _Locktype = kind;
    if (kind < _MAX_LOCK) EnterCriticalSection(&mtx[kind]);

The table is four `CRITICAL_SECTION`s - the `0x18` stride is `sizeof(RTL_CRITICAL_SECTION)` on 32 bit - and `0xB958D8`, the thing it calls, is a one-line forwarder to the `EnterCriticalSection` import. `__thiscall`, `ret 4`, the object back in eax (constructors do). 47 callers, paired one for one with 47 callers of the destructor below.

The lock ids seen in use are `0` from `std::locale::Destruct` (`_LOCK_LOCALE`) and `2` from `std::ios_base::_Addstd` (`_LOCK_STREAM`).

**The real `basic_stringbuf<char>::str()`, and its address is rva `0x520940` - not `0x920940`.** `0x920940` is the *virtual* address the disassembler prints inside `std::ostringstream::str` (`0x65BD10`); the handover carried it across as an rva. Rva `0x920940` (VA `0xD20940`) is not a function entry at all: it decodes from the middle of an instruction and has zero callers. This is the trap `findings/README.md` warns about, and the previous wave's own `frontier` list did record it correctly as `0x520940`.

The body is MSVC's `str()` unchanged:

    if (!(_Mystate & _Constant) && pptr() != 0)
        return string(pbase(), max(_Seekhigh, pptr()) - pbase());
    else if (!(_Mystate & _Noread) && gptr() != 0)
        return string(eback(), egptr() - eback());
    else
        return string();

All three arms build a local `Hoi3CString` with `std::string::assign` (`0xA160`), tidy `out` to the empty state (`[out+0x14]=0xF`, `[out+0x10]=0`, `[out]=0`) and copy the local into it with `0x32720`, then free the local when its capacity reached 0x10.

**Field offsets, streambuf-relative** (MSVC keeps pointers *to* the area pointers, so each is dereferenced twice): `+0x10` `_IGfirst`, `+0x14` `_IPfirst`, `+0x20` `_IGnext`, `+0x24` `_IPnext`, `+0x30` `_IGcount`; `egptr()` is computed as `*_IGnext + *_IGcount`. `+0x3C` is `_Seekhigh` and `+0x40` `_Mystate`, which is the cross-check that settles the identification: `LogRecord` puts its stringbuf at `record+4` and the already-confirmed `std::ostringstream::ostringstream` writes `_Seekhigh` at `record+0x40` and `_Mystate` at `record+0x44` - the same two fields, the same two offsets.

**Convention.** `esi` is never pushed, never assigned and never restored, and the epilogue is `mov eax,esi` - so it is an incoming argument, the named-return-value slot. `ecx` is the buffer. Bare `ret`, nothing on the stack. Both callers set it up that way: `std::ostringstream::str` does `mov esi,eax; add ecx,4; call` and the second caller, `0x51FB50`, does `mov esi,eax; add ecx,0x18; call`.

The state bits are `_Constant = 2` and `_Noread = 4`, tested in that order.
